==English==

===Etymology===
Probably from {{bor|en|yi|שנויץ||snout}}, cognate to {{cog|de|Schnauze|t=snout, muzzle}} and {{cog|en|snout}}. The ''Oxford English Dictionary'' suggests the word may be pseudo-Yiddish coined in English. Attested since 1930.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-schnozzle.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒzəl|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} The human [[nose]], especially one that is large.
#: {{syn|en|beak|[[conk]] {{q|British}}|[[honker]] {{q|US}}|[[hooter]] {{q|British}}|[[schnoz]], [[schnozz]], [[shnoz]], [[schnozzola]]|[[snoot]] {{q|US}}|Thesaurus:nose}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1932|title=The Passionate Plumber|journal=Motion Picture|page=64|text=Buster Keaton is the plumber, passionate or otherwise, and he is more than ably assisted by the '''Schnozzle''', Jimmy Durante.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1947|author=Leslie Waller|title=Show Me the Way|page=31|text=I poked that bastard in the '''schnozzle''', he told himself proudly, and everybody on the crew will thank me.}}

